%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 118899cb856d7e04bac83f4463fd3b31, type: 3}
  m_Name: Scene08Dialogue
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::AdaptiveOhmsLaw.DialogueSequence
  lines:
  - id: intro
    speaker: WATTSON
    text: These are the measurements you collected. Let's see their pattern on a
      graph.
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: ead9403d5b858ba438fd0415c0721b08, type: 3}
    duration: 5.3284583
    emotion: Curious
    nextEvent: 
    overrideCinematicSubject: 0
    cinematicSubject: 0
  - id: sample
    speaker: WATTSON
    text: We are using clearly marked practice readings from an ideal five-ohm circuit
      at constant temperature.
    audio: {fileID: 0}
    duration: 6
    emotion: Curious
    nextEvent: 
    overrideCinematicSubject: 0
    cinematicSubject: 0
  - id: axes
    speaker: WATTSON
    text: Current goes along the bottom. Potential difference goes up the side. Select
      a reading, then tap where its two values meet.
    audio: {fileID: 0}
    duration: 8.958208
    emotion: Curious
    nextEvent: 
    overrideCinematicSubject: 0
    cinematicSubject: 0
  - id: placement_hint
    speaker: WATTSON
    text: Let's follow one value at a time. Find the current column, then the voltage
      row.
    audio: {fileID: 0}
    duration: 6.5236964
    emotion: Curious
    nextEvent: 
    overrideCinematicSubject: 0
    cinematicSubject: 0
  - id: pattern
    speaker: WATTSON
    text: Your five points form a straight line. In this ideal model, zero volts
      also gives zero current, so the line passes through the origin.
    audio: {fileID: 0}
    duration: 9.6
    emotion: Curious
    nextEvent: 
    overrideCinematicSubject: 0
    cinematicSubject: 0
  - id: question
    speaker: WATTSON
    text: What does this straight line through the origin tell us, for this fixed
      resistor?
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 1a677f5d32558e746b8a9b2d4d6885ad, type: 3}
    duration: 5.3436055
    emotion: Curious
    nextEvent: 
    overrideCinematicSubject: 0
    cinematicSubject: 0
  - id: question_hint
    speaker: WATTSON
    text: At one volt we measured 0.20 amperes. At two volts we measured 0.40 amperes.
      Both values doubled. What relationship fits?
    audio: {fileID: 0}
    duration: 8
    emotion: Curious
    nextEvent: 
    overrideCinematicSubject: 0
    cinematicSubject: 0
  - id: conclusion
    speaker: WATTSON
    text: 'The evidence shows proportionality: doubling voltage doubles current.
      This holds for our fixed resistor at constant temperature.'
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: bd49d1041cdd5444a8c472781870b747, type: 3}
    duration: 8.972766
    emotion: Curious
    nextEvent: 
    overrideCinematicSubject: 0
    cinematicSubject: 0
  - id: complete
    speaker: WATTSON
    text: Your graph and explanation are saved. We have a pattern we can use in our
      next investigation.
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 98757c09e8419d34e88c9331d3f57ef3, type: 3}
    duration: 6.8434696
    emotion: Curious
    nextEvent: 
    overrideCinematicSubject: 0
    cinematicSubject: 0
